  "content": {
    "id": "p11-ch17-q4-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "4",
    "prompt": "One more than a number is the same as 42 divided by the number. What can the number be?",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$-7$ or $6$.",
      "worked": "Let the number be $x$, where $x\\ne0$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}x+1&=\\frac{42}{x}\\\\x(x+1)&=42\\\\x^2+x-42&=0\\\\(x+7)(x-6)&=0\\end{align*}$$\n\nBy the null factor law, $x+7=0$ or $x-6=0$, so $x=-7$ or $x=6$.\n\nCheck: $-7+1=-6=42\\div(-7)$ and $6+1=7=42\\div6$. Both values satisfy the statement.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 49
  },
